.Dd August 23, 2026
.Dt SE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm se
.Nd search and rewrite structural byte ranges
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl E
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl i Ns Op = Ns Ar suffix
.Op Fl w
.Ar program
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl -print-man
.Nm
.Fl -install-man Ns Op = Ns Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a structural text editor.
It runs a small command language over byte ranges called views.
A view may be a line, a regular-expression match, a field, a quoted string,
a balanced bracketed range, or the complete input.
Commands select views, reject views, print them, or record replacements.
.Pp
With no
.Ar file ,
.Nm
reads standard input.
Named files are processed in argument order.
A program beginning with
.Ql /
is applied as a predicate to line views.
Every other program begins with one view spanning the complete input.
.Pp
Selectors do not print by themselves.
Use
.Cm p
to print a view or
.Cm =
to print its byte coordinates.
A mutating program writes the transformed document after all commands have
finished.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl E
Accepted for compatibility.
Extended regular-expression syntax is already enabled.
.It Fl i Ns Op = Ns Ar suffix
Rewrite each named file atomically.
Standard input is not allowed with this option.
When
.Ar suffix
is present, copy the original to
.Pa file Ns Ar suffix
before replacing it.
.It Fl n
Accepted for sed compatibility.
.Nm
does not print implicitly, so this option does not change output.
.It Fl w
Run the program again when a named file changes.
The current watcher uses kqueue and is available on macOS only.
.It Fl -print-man
Write the roff source of this manual to standard output and exit.
.It Fl -install-man Ns Op = Ns Ar directory
Install
.Pa se.1
under
.Ar directory .
Without a directory, use
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1
or
.Pa $HOME/.local/share/man/man1 .
.It Fl -
End option parsing.
Use this before a
.Ar program
whose first command is the collapse operator
.Cm - .
.El
.Sh PROGRAM MODEL
Commands are separated by whitespace or semicolons.
Whitespace inside a delimited pattern or an awk block belongs to that
construct.
The initial view is normally the complete input.
Selectors replace the current view stream with another stream.
Blocks run a nested program for each incoming view.
Mutations retain absolute byte coordinates and are applied to the original
input after execution.
.Pp
Two rules account for most command behavior:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Reporting pipelines need
.Cm p
or
.Cm = .
.It
Mutating pipelines write the document and normally should not end in
.Cm p .
.El
.Sh PATTERNS
A pattern command uses the next character as its delimiter.
The slash is conventional but not special.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
x/pattern/
s/pattern/replacement/
s#path/with/slashes#replacement#
.Ed
.Pp
Escape a delimiter inside a field with a backslash.
Patterns use the regex-automata syntax: literals, alternation, groups,
repetition, ASCII and Unicode classes, named captures, word boundaries, and
inline flags are supported.
Look-around and pattern backreferences are not supported.
Replacement references are handled separately and are supported.
.Pp
The following flags follow the closing delimiter:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm i , I
Case-insensitive matching.
.It Cm s
Allow dot to match a newline.
.It Cm g
For
.Cm s ,
replace all eligible matches in each view.
.It Cm m , M
Accepted; line anchors are multiline by default.
.It Ar number
For
.Cm s ,
begin with that one-based occurrence.
.El
.Sh SELECTORS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm x/re/
Replace each input view with its non-overlapping matches.
.It Cm y/re/ , z/re/
Split each view at matches and keep the non-empty gaps.
.It Cm g/re/
Keep a view when it contains a match.
.It Cm v/re/
Reject a view when it contains a match.
.It Cm ~k/literal/
Keep views containing a substring within Levenshtein distance
.Ar k
of the literal.
.It Cm :sem:/concept/
Keep views matching a small built-in related-term lexicon.
An unknown concept becomes a case-insensitive substring search.
.El
.Sh MUTATIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm c/text/
Replace each complete view with literal text.
Use
.Cm c//
to delete a selected view.
.It Cm s/re/replacement/flags
Replace the first match in each view unless a global or numeric flag says
otherwise.
.El
.Pp
Replacement fields understand these forms:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm $0 , ${0} , & , \e0
The complete match.
.It Cm $1 No through Cm $99 , ${12}
A numbered capture.
.It Cm $name , ${name}
A named capture.
.It Cm \e1 No through Cm \e9
A sed-style numbered capture.
.It Cm $$
A literal dollar sign.
.It Cm \e&
A literal ampersand.
.It Cm \en , \et , \er , \e\e , \exNN
Control characters, a backslash, or one byte written in hexadecimal.
.El
.Sh ACTIONS AND CONTROL
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm p
Print each view.
Add a newline when the view does not already end in one.
.It Cm =
Print
.Dq start,end,length
using zero-based, half-open byte coordinates.
.It Cm { program }
Run a nested program for every incoming view.
.It Cm /re/ { program }
Run the block on matching line views and pass other lines through unchanged.
Without a block, print matching lines.
.It Cm m/re/ { program }
Extract every match and run the block on each match in order.
.It Cm t { program }
Run the block if a previous substitution changed its view.
The test consumes the change flag.
.It Cm N
Join adjacent pairs of views.
An odd final view passes through unchanged.
.It Cm r/separator/
Reduce all current views to one value separated by literal
.Ar separator .
.It Cm +
Widen a view to the smallest enclosing balanced bracket range.
.It Cm -
Replace a view with the contents of its first balanced bracket pair.
.It Cm @{ awk-program }
Run the bundled awk action with each incoming view as one record.
.El
.Sh AWK ACTION
The awk action is intended for fields, counters, arrays, arithmetic, and
formatted reports after structural selection.
It supports
.Cm BEGIN
and
.Cm END
blocks, ordinary statements, fields
.Cm $0
through
.Cm $NF ,
computed fields, variables, associative arrays, and control flow.
.Pp
The predefined record values are
.Cm NR
and
.Cm NF .
The field and output separators are
.Cm FS , OFS ,
and
.Cm ORS .
A default
.Cm FS
of one space splits on runs of Unicode whitespace and trims the ends.
A one-character or multi-character separator is literal.
.Pp
Supported control statements are
.Cm if , else , while , for , break , continue ,
and
.Cm next .
Associative arrays may be traversed with
.Ql for (key in array) .
Supported string functions include
.Cm length , substr , index , tolower , toupper , split ,
and
.Cm sprintf .
Numeric functions include
.Cm sin , cos , atan2 , exp , log , sqrt , int , rand ,
and
.Cm srand .
.Pp
This is not a complete POSIX awk.
It does not implement regex-valued expressions, user functions,
.Cm getline ,
redirection, or the full set of special variables.
Use a full awk implementation when a program needs those features.
.Sh LINE RECORDS
Use
.Ql x/.*\en?/
for ordinary Unix line records.
The optional newline keeps a final unterminated record.
Use
.Ql x/.*\en/
only when unterminated final bytes should be ignored.
.Pp
For CRLF data, a line view ends in carriage return and newline.
Patterns anchored at the end commonly need
.Ql \er?$
to accept both LF and CRLF.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory used by
.Fl -install-man
when no destination is supplied.
.It Ev HOME
Used for
.Pa $HOME/.local/share/man/man1
when
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/se.1
Default per-user manual path when
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
is set.
.It Pa $HOME/.local/share/man/man1/se.1
Default per-user manual path otherwise.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std se
A successful no-match search exits zero and prints nothing.
This differs from grep, where status 1 means no selected lines.
Parse errors, invalid regular expressions, input errors, and runtime awk errors
produce a non-zero status and a diagnostic on standard error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Find lines
.\" tested: readme.search
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'ready\enERROR disk full\enretry\en' | se '/error/i'
ERROR disk full
.Ed
.Pp
Reject matching lines by making line records explicit:
.\" tested: grep.invert
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '# heading\envalue=1\en\envalue=2\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ v/^#/ p'
value=1

value=2
.Ed
.Pp
Print only matching parts:
.\" tested: grep.matches
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'from a@example.org to bob.smith@example.net\en' |
> se 'x/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\e.[A-Za-z]{2,}/ p'
a@example.org
bob.smith@example.net
.Ed
.Pp
Count matching lines after filtering:
.\" tested: grep.count
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'ok\enerror one\enERROR two\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ g/error/i @{ END { print NR } }'
2
.Ed
.Pp
Print match byte offsets:
.\" tested: grep.offset
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'a needle and needle\en' | se 'x/needle/ ='
2,8,6
13,19,6
.Ed
.Pp
Find a NUL byte without printing it:
.\" tested: grep.nul
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'left\e0right' | se 'x/\ex00/ ='
4,5,1
.Ed
.Ss Rewrite text
Replace every occurrence in the complete input:
.\" tested: sed.global
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'foo foo\enfoo\en' | se 's/foo/bar/g'
bar bar
bar
.Ed
.Pp
Replace the first occurrence on each line:
.\" tested: sed.first_each_line
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'foo foo\enfoo foo\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ { s/foo/bar/ }'
bar foo
bar foo
.Ed
.Pp
Use another delimiter for slash-heavy paths:
.\" tested: sed.path
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin\en' |
> se 's#/usr/local#/opt/se#g'
PATH=/opt/se/bin:/opt/se/sbin
.Ed
.Pp
Reorder capture groups:
.\" tested: sed.capture
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'from 2026-08-22 to 2026-09-01\en' |
> se 's/([0-9]{4})-([0-9]{2})-([0-9]{2})/$3\e/$2\e/$1/g'
from 22/08/2026 to 01/09/2026
.Ed
.Pp
Redact simple key-value fields:
.\" tested: sed.redact
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'token=abc&user=7 password=hunter2\en' |
> se 's/(token|password)=[^ &\en]+/$1=REDACTED/g'
token=REDACTED&user=7 password=REDACTED
.Ed
.Pp
Delete comments and blank lines:
.\" tested: sed.delete
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '# comment\en\enport=8080\en  # note\enhost=localhost\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ { g/^\es*(#|$)/ c// }'
port=8080
host=localhost
.Ed
.Pp
Edit only addressed lines:
.\" tested: sed.address
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'stable enabled=true\endeprecated enabled=true\en' |
> se '/deprecated/ { s/enabled=true/enabled=false/g }'
stable enabled=true
deprecated enabled=false
.Ed
.Pp
Replace a section spanning several lines:
.\" tested: sed.multiline
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'keep\enBEGIN\ensecret\enEND\enkeep\en' |
> se 's/BEGIN.*END/[section removed]/s'
keep
[section removed]
keep
.Ed
.Ss Field reports
Print the record number, field count, first field, and last field:
.\" tested: awk.fields
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'alpha beta gamma\enone two\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ @{ print NR, NF, $1, $NF }'
1 3 alpha gamma
2 2 one two
.Ed
.Pp
Sum a column:
.\" tested: awk.sum
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'apples 12\enpears 8\enplums 5\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ @{ sum += $2 END { print sum } }'
25
.Ed
.Pp
Compute an average with a fixed format:
.\" tested: awk.average
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'a 10\enb 20\enc 25\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ @{ sum += $2 END { printf "%.2f\en", sum/NR } }'
18.33
.Ed
.Pp
Count named categories:
.\" tested: awk.count
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'GET /\enPOST /login\enGET /health\enGET /\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ @{ count[$1]++ END { print count["GET"], count["POST"] } }'
3 1
.Ed
.Pp
Find the minimum and maximum without assuming zero is in the data:
.\" tested: awk.minmax
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '%s\en' -4 12 3 -9 |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ @{ if (NR==1 || $1<min) min=$1; if (NR==1 || $1>max) max=$1 END { print min, max } }'
-9 12
.Ed
.Ss Structural selection
Select the bracketed object containing a key:
.\" tested: structural.json
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '{"server":{"timeout":30,"retry":2},"client":{"retry":1}}\en' |
> se 'x/timeout/ + p'
{"timeout":30,"retry":2}
.Ed
.Pp
Find likely misspellings within edit distance two:
.\" tested: structural.fuzzy
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'kubernetes\enkubernets\enkuberntes\endocker\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ ~2/kubernetes/ p'
kubernetes
kubernets
kuberntes
.Ed
.Pp
Search the built-in network concept:
.\" tested: structural.semantic
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'CPU is hot\enDNS lookup failed\ensocket closed\en' |
> se 'x/.*\en?/ :sem:/network/ p'
DNS lookup failed
socket closed
.Ed
.Sh FILE SELECTION
.Nm
does not traverse directories or read ignore files.
Pass named files directly, use
.Xr find 1
with
.Cm -exec ,
or use
.Xr rg 1
with
.Cm --files -0
and a NUL-safe shell loop.
The command does not add file-name prefixes to output.
.Pp
Decompress archives before passing them to standard input, for example with
.Xr gzip 1
or
.Xr xz 1 .
An in-place edit cannot operate on a decompression pipeline.
.Sh IN-PLACE EDITS
Use a backup suffix on the first pass:
.\" integration-tested: cli.in_place_edit_with_backup
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ se -i=.bak 's/old/new/g' config
$ cat config.bak config
old=1
new=1
.Ed
.Pp
The replacement is written to a sibling temporary file, synchronized, and
renamed over the original.
The original file mode is preserved.
A file with no mutations is left untouched.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
The regular-expression engine does not support look-around or pattern
backreferences.
Tree navigation counts brackets and does not parse strings or comments.
The concept selector uses a static lexicon, not contextual classification.
Recursive traversal, file-name labels, source line numbers, surrounding
context, and grep's three-state exit contract remain jobs for other tools.
The awk action omits several POSIX and GNU awk features described above.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr awk 1 ,
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr rg 1 ,
.Xr sed 1
.Pp
